    <section style="background:#dfe2d4">
      <p class="eyebrow">Application matters</p>
      <h2>How to wear mineral sunscreen comfortably</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>1. Start with moisturized skin</h3>
        <p>Apply your usual moisturizer and let it settle. This can help sunscreen spread more evenly over dry areas.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>2. Apply enough product</h3>
        <p>Use the amount directed on the product label. Applying too little can reduce the protection you receive compared with the labeled SPF.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>3. Blend in sections</h3>
        <p>Spread sunscreen over one area at a time, including your forehead, cheeks, nose, jaw, ears, and neck. Avoid leaving gaps near your hairline.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>4. Reapply throughout the day</h3>
        <p>Reapply at least every two hours outdoors and after swimming or sweating, following the label. Seek shade and use protective clothing, especially around midday.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Frequently asked questions</p>
      <h2>Mineral sunscreen, made simpler</h2>
      <h3>Is mineral sunscreen always better for sensitive skin?</h3>
      <p>Not always. Individual skin responses vary, and a formula's full ingredient list matters. If you have sensitive skin, introduce products carefully and discontinue use if irritation develops.</p>
      <h3>Does tinted sunscreen provide enough protection?</h3>
      <p>A tinted sunscreen can provide sun protection if it is properly formulated, tested, and labeled. Apply it according to its directions and do not rely on tint alone as sun protection.</p>
      <h3>Can I wear makeup over mineral sunscreen?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Let sunscreen settle before applying makeup, and avoid rubbing so much that you disturb the protective layer. Reapply sunscreen as directed throughout the day.</p>
      <h3>Can I use sunscreen only when it is very sunny?</h3>
      <p>UV exposure can occur on cloudy days, too. Make broad-spectrum sun protection part of your daily routine when the UV index indicates a need for protection, and follow local sun-safety guidance.</p>
